
  - [1mPure rules only[0m — the module exports validators ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, …) and a [38;5;153mvalidate(values, rules)[39m that returns
    [38;5;153m{valid, errors}[39m. It never touches the DOM. Form #2 still writes its own listener and its own value-reading. Cheapest
    to build, trivial to test with no DOM, and it composes into either of the options below later. Costs you: the
    per-form boilerplate stays duplicated, so "reusable" only covers the rules, not the wiring.
  - [1mRules + form binding[0m — additionally a [38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, rules, onValid)[39m that reads named inputs, validates on submit,
    and hands you clean values. Form #2 becomes a rules object and a callback, which is the real payoff. Costs you: it
    needs a DOM convention (inputs addressed by [38;5;153mname[39m, not the literal ids [38;5;153mindex.html[39m uses today), so it forces a small
    HTML change, and testing needs a DOM shim.
  - [1mRules + binding + error rendering[0m — also injects error messages into the page. Most complete, but it commits you to
    a markup and styling convention for errors with zero real forms to validate that convention against. This is the one
    I think you'd regret.

  Cheap to change later: adding binding on top of pure rules, and adding new rule types. Expensive to change later: the
  shape of the rules object and the [38;5;153m{valid, errors}[39m contract, because every form will encode against it; and error
  rendering, because it leaks into your HTML.

  [1mMy recommendation: rules + form binding.[0m Pure-rules-only is the safer-sounding answer, but it doesn't actually deliver
  what you asked for — the duplicated part of a new form is mostly the wiring, not the [38;5;153mif[39m statements, so form #2 would
  still be a copy-paste job. Binding is where the reuse lives. I'd leave error rendering out until a form exists that
  needs it.
